SELECT 'b97b4eb6-bd20-7fe2-881a-26eb30a19029', '5281d3a7-7442-74fe-b147-d25a4886608c', 'Resolving Conflicts', 'Resolving Conflicts', 'What happens when Git cannot merge automatically, and how to resolve it manually.
Merge Conflict
A Merge Conflict is an event that occurs when Git tries to merge two branches that have conflicting changes in the same line of the same file, or when a file has been deleted in one branch but modified in the other. Git cannot decide which version to keep and pauses the merge.
How Git Flags Conflicts
When a conflict occurs, Git marks the conflict boundaries directly in the affected files using conflict markers:
<<<<<<< HEAD
This is my local code change on main branch.
=======
This is the incoming code change from feature branch.
>>>>>>> feature-branch
Step-by-Step Conflict Resolution
Open the conflicted files in an editor (VS Code highlights these sections).
Inspect the code and manually choose which changes to keep, removing the markers (<<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>>).
Stage the resolved files: git add [filename].
Finalize the merge by committing: git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict".',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Resolving Conflicts'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Resolving Conflicts'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'What happens when Git cannot merge automatically, and how to resolve it manually.
Merge Conflict
A Merge Conflict is an event that occurs when Git tries to merge two branches that have conflicting changes in the same line of the same file, or when a file has been deleted in one branch but modified in the other. Git cannot decide which version to keep and pauses the merge.
How Git Flags Conflicts
When a conflict occurs, Git marks the conflict boundaries directly in the affected files using conflict markers:
<<<<<<< HEAD
This is my local code change on main branch.
=======
This is the incoming code change from feature branch.
>>>>>>> feature-branch
Step-by-Step Conflict Resolution
Open the conflicted files in an editor (VS Code highlights these sections).
Inspect the code and manually choose which changes to keep, removing the markers (<<<<<<<, =======, >>>>>>>).
Stage the resolved files: git add [filename].
Finalize the merge by committing: git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict".'), 'C'),
  TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 03:23:53+00'
