SELECT '18ac625f-6135-7341-a3af-cdd9ad62c2ee', '53df94ba-d473-7c49-851f-6c6f4ac7c50b', 'Pushing Code', 'Pushing Code', 'Uploading committed snapshots from your local machine to the remote repository.
Push
Pushing is the process of uploading your local repository commit snapshots to a remote repository server like GitHub, making your local history available to collaborators.
Setting Up Upstream & Pushing
The first time you push code from a new local branch, you must bind it to the remote branch using the upstream flag (-u):
# Push and set upstream destination
git push -u origin main
For subsequent pushes, once the upstream is configured, you can run the simplified command:
git push
Analogy: Publishing a Draft
Analogy
Writing code is like writing a blog post. Saving drafts is like committing locally on your laptop (private). Pushing is like hitting "Publish" or syncing it to your cloud drive, making it public to the internet so others can read and edit it.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Pushing Code'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Pushing Code'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Uploading committed snapshots from your local machine to the remote repository.
Push
Pushing is the process of uploading your local repository commit snapshots to a remote repository server like GitHub, making your local history available to collaborators.
Setting Up Upstream & Pushing
The first time you push code from a new local branch, you must bind it to the remote branch using the upstream flag (-u):
# Push and set upstream destination
git push -u origin main
For subsequent pushes, once the upstream is configured, you can run the simplified command:
git push
Analogy: Publishing a Draft
Analogy
Writing code is like writing a blog post. Saving drafts is like committing locally on your laptop (private). Pushing is like hitting "Publish" or syncing it to your cloud drive, making it public to the internet so others can read and edit it.'), 'C'),
  TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 03:23:53+00'
