LCV — least constraining value	Try the value that rules out the fewest choices for neighbours	Fail last: leave maximum flexibility — we only need one solution
Inference: forward checking and arc consistency
Forward checking
After assigning X = v, delete v-inconsistent values from the domains of X''s unassigned neighbours. If a domain becomes empty, backtrack immediately.
Arc consistency
Arc X → Y is consistent if every value of X has some supporting value in Y. AC-3 enforces this for all arcs, propagating deletions until nothing changes.
MAC
Maintaining arc consistency: run AC-3 after every assignment during backtracking — stronger than forward checking.
def solve(n, mrv=False, forward_check=False):
    nodes = 0
    def backtrack(assign, domains):
        nonlocal nodes
        if len(assign) == n:
            return dict(assign)
        free = [c for c in range(n) if c not in assign]
        var = min(free, key=lambda c: len(domains[c])) if mrv else free[0]
        for row in sorted(domains[var]):
            if any(row == r or abs(row - r) == abs(var - c) for c, r in assign.items()):
                continue
            nodes += 1
            assign[var] = row
            new = domains
            if forward_check:              # prune neighbours'' domains; empty domain → dead end
