SELECT '1433cd31-636d-7937-a4f8-c8ad282ce60b', '436d9ab5-161c-7ce4-98ec-4dab97deef90', 'Designing Heuristics', 'Designing Heuristics', 'A* is only as good as its heuristic. Good heuristics are admissible, as close to the true cost as possible, and cheap to compute — and the standard way to invent one is to relax the problem.
Two classic 8-puzzle heuristics
Heuristic	Definition	Relaxed problem it solves exactly
h₁ — misplaced tiles	Number of tiles not in their goal square	A tile can jump anywhere in one move
h₂ — Manhattan distance	Sum over tiles of |row − goal row| + |col − goal col|	A tile can move to any adjacent square, even an occupied one
A relaxed problem has more actions, so its optimal cost can only be lower — that is why a heuristic derived from a relaxation is automatically admissible (and consistent).
import heapq

GOAL = (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)         # 0 = blank

def h1(s):                                  # misplaced tiles (the blank doesn''t count)
    return sum(1 for i, tile in enumerate(s) if tile and tile != i)

def h2(s):                                  # Manhattan distance
    return sum(abs(i // 3 - t // 3) + abs(i % 3 - t % 3) for i, t in enumerate(s) if t)

def neighbours(s):
    i = s.index(0)
    r, c = divmod(i, 3)
    for dr, dc in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):
        if 0 <= r + dr < 3 and 0 <= c + dc < 3:
            j = (r + dr) * 3 + c + dc
            t = list(s)
            t[i], t[j] = t[j], t[i]
            yield tuple(t)

def astar(start, h):
    frontier, best, expanded = [(h(start), 0, start)], {start: 0}, 0
    while frontier:
        f, g, s = heapq.heappop(frontier)
        if s == GOAL:
            return g, expanded
        if g > best[s]:
            continue
        expanded += 1
        for t in neighbours(s):
            if g + 1 < best.get(t, 99):
                best[t] = g + 1
                heapq.heappush(frontier, (g + 1 + h(t), g + 1, t))

start = (7, 2, 4,
         5, 0, 6,
         8, 3, 1)
print(h1(start), h2(start))                 # 8 18 ← true cost is 26
print(astar(start, lambda s: 0))            # (26, 162240) ← h = 0 is uniform-cost search
print(astar(start, h1))                     # (26, 44988)
print(astar(start, h2))                     # (26, 4085)
All three find the optimal 26-move solution; Manhattan distance does it with roughly 40× fewer expansions than no heuristic.
Dominance and the effective branching factor
Dominance — if h₂(n) ≥ h₁(n) for every n and both are admissible, h₂ dominates h₁ and A* with h₂ never expands more nodes. Larger is better — as long as it stays admissible.
To compare heuristics independently of problem size, compute the effective branching factor b*: the branching factor a uniform tree of depth d would need to contain the N nodes A* expanded. Close to 1 is ideal.
N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + … + (b*)ᵈ
def effective_branching(n_expanded, depth):
    lo, hi = 1.0, 10.0
    for _ in range(60):                        # bisection
        b = (lo + hi) / 2
        total = sum(b ** i for i in range(depth + 1))
        lo, hi = (b, hi) if total < n_expanded + 1 else (lo, b)
    return round(b, 2)

print(effective_branching(162240, 26))     # 1.52 ← no heuristic
print(effective_branching(44988, 26))      # 1.44 ← misplaced tiles
print(effective_branching(4085, 26))       # 1.3 ← Manhattan distance
Recipes for heuristics
Relax the problem
Remove constraints until the problem is easy to solve exactly: straight-line distance (ignore roads), Manhattan distance (ignore other tiles), ignore-preconditions in planning.
Max of admissible heuristics
h(n) = max(h₁(n), …, hₖ(n)) is admissible and dominates each component — use it when no single one is best everywhere.
Pattern databases
Precompute exact costs for a sub-problem (e.g. tiles 1–4 only) for every configuration and look them up. Disjoint patterns can be added. Solves the 15-puzzle and Rubik''s cube quickly.
Learn a heuristic
Train a model to predict cost-to-go from features of a state (AlphaGo''s value network is a learned evaluation). Fast, but no admissibility guarantee.
Domain	Admissible heuristic
Grid, 4-way moves	Manhattan distance |dx| + |dy|
Grid, 8-way moves (diagonal cost √2)	Octile distance max(dx, dy) + (√2 − 1)·min(dx, dy)
Any-angle / road maps	Euclidean (straight-line) distance
Travelling salesperson	Cost of a minimum spanning tree of unvisited cities
Watch out
Manhattan distance is not admissible on an 8-connected grid — one diagonal step (cost √2 ≈ 1.41) covers Manhattan distance 2, so it overestimates.
What makes a heuristic admissible? Give one for the 8-puzzle.
It never overestimates the true cost to the goal. Manhattan distance: every tile must move at least its row and column offset, one square per move, so the sum can''t exceed the real number of moves.
Admissible vs consistent?
Admissible: h(n) ≤ h*(n). Consistent: h(n) ≤ c(n, n′) + h(n′) for every edge — a stronger condition that implies admissibility and guarantees f is non-decreasing along paths, so graph-search A* never needs to re-open nodes.
If h₂ dominates h₁, is h₂ always better?
It never expands more nodes, but it may cost more to compute per node. The best heuristic balances accuracy against evaluation time.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Designing Heuristics'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Designing Heuristics'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'A* is only as good as its heuristic. Good heuristics are admissible, as close to the true cost as possible, and cheap to compute — and the standard way to invent one is to relax the problem.
