INSERT INTO "notes"."NoteSearchDocument" ("noteId", "versionId", "title", "topics", "body", "document", "updatedAt")
SELECT 'eda16518-9da8-710a-8319-e1ce1add33b5', '1a5dc92f-e229-70ee-b46a-17184879e277', 'AI Architecture', 'AI Architecture', 'How to design large-scale infrastructure for GenAI applications.
LLM APIs vs Local Models
- APIs (OpenAI, Gemini): Serverless, state-of-the-art reasoning, no hosting overhead. High variable cost, rate limits, data privacy/compliance concerns.
 - Local (Llama, Mistral): Hosted on private GPUs. Fixed cost, data privacy control, low latency (if cached). Requires complex infrastructure setup and GPU maintenance.
Latency & GPU Inference
LLM latency is broken into: 
- Time-to-First-Token (TTFT): Time taken to process the input prompt (prefill phase). Compute bound (all prompt tokens processed in parallel). 
- Inter-Token Latency (ITL): Time taken to generate subsequent tokens autoregressively. Memory-bandwidth bound (weights and KV cache are re-read for every token).
Operational Optimizations
Streaming
Pushing tokens to the client via Server-Sent Events (SSE) as they are generated, rather than waiting for the entire sequence to complete. This drastically reduces perceived latency for the user.
Prompt Caching
Caching the key-value states of long prefix prompts (like system prompts or large context segments) on the inference server (or by the API provider). If a subsequent request shares the same prefix, we skip the prefill calculations, cutting TTFT and API costs by up to 90%.
Tool Calling / Function Calling
The model outputs structured arguments (JSON) indicating a function it wants to invoke (e.g., get_weather(location="Seattle")). The backend executes the function, feeds the result back to the model, and the model synthesizes the final response.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'AI Architecture'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'AI Architecture'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'How to design large-scale infrastructure for GenAI applications.
LLM APIs vs Local Models
- APIs (OpenAI, Gemini): Serverless, state-of-the-art reasoning, no hosting overhead. High variable cost, rate limits, data privacy/compliance concerns.
 - Local (Llama, Mistral): Hosted on private GPUs. Fixed cost, data privacy control, low latency (if cached). Requires complex infrastructure setup and GPU maintenance.
