ence model designed to process and synthesize human language. They utilize millions or billions of parameters and are built on the Transformer architecture. Trained on massive corpora of crawled internet text, code, and books, they learn syntax, context, and reasoning paths, allowing them to predict the next word or token in a sequence to engage in dialog, write code, and summarize documents."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"84cf5b26-2cb0-7c72-b016-56639878d404","type":"question","position":"a5","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"What is a Token?\""}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fast Answer:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The basic atomic unit of text (word, subword, or character) processed by an LLM."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A token is a piece of text (word, subword or character) that the LLM processes, each mapped to a numeric token ID. Tokenizers break text into subword segments (e.g., the word \"unbelievable\" might be split into three tokens: [\"un\", \"believ\", \"able\"]). These tokens are converted into numbers (token IDs) referencing the model''s vocabulary list. On average, 1 token is roughly equivalent to 4 characters or 0.75 words in English."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"d85e58d6-f701-775b-8125-8a6a97e4a78f","type":"question","position":"a6","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"What is Hallucination?\""}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fast Answer:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" When an LLM generates text that is factually incorrect, ungrounded, or fabricated but outputs it with absolute confidence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hallucinations occur because LLMs are not database lookup engines; they are statistical next-token prediction machines. They generate content that matches the training data distribution but is not verified for absolute truth. Common causes include ungrounded prompts, contradictory training data, or the model prioritizing smooth linguistic flow over factual verification."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"551cbc77-563f-764c-88b5-25474aa0bb5b","type":"question","position":"a7","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"What is Temperature?\""}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fast Answer:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" A parameter adjusting token selection randomness: 0 makes outputs deterministic; 1+ makes them random and creative."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Temperature controls how probabilities are distributed across token choices prior to sampling during inference. It scales the raw logits output by the neural network. Setting temperature to 0 forces the model to pick only the single most probable token (greedy decoding), making outputs far more consistent (though not always bit-for-bit reproducible on hosted APIs). Setting it higher flattens the probability curve, giving less-likely tokens a chance to be picked, resulting in creative, varied, or random outputs."}]}]}},"revision":1},{"id":"dc92537c-1a7d-70e4-9a40-4abb7496b54c","type":"question","position":"a8","content":{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"What is Prompt Engineering?\""}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fast Answer:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The practice of formatting and styling input prompts to guide an LLM to generate optimal, structured responses."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prompt Engineering is the active design of input instructions, context const