SELECT '916feb8a-1cf6-7524-babf-681deef2fd5b', 'cda04cdd-bc49-793e-b382-5281b7728c29', 'What Is Machine Learning?', 'What Is Machine Learning?', 'Machine learning builds programs that improve at a task from data instead of from hand-written rules. You supply examples; a learning algorithm finds the parameters of a model that generalises to examples it has never seen.
Machine learning (Mitchell, 1997) — a program learns from experience E with respect to a task T and a performance measure P if its performance at T, measured by P, improves with E. Spam filtering: T = label emails, E = emails people marked as spam or not, P = the share of emails labelled correctly.
Rules vs learning
In traditional programming a person writes the rules. In ML the rules — the model''s parameters — are fitted from examples of inputs paired with the right answers.
Data + hand-written rules → Program → Answers
Data + known answers → Learning algorithm → Model (learned rules) → Answers for new data
A tiny example: the algorithm is never told the rule y = 3x + 2; it recovers it from 50 noisy examples.
import numpy as np
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
X = rng.uniform(0, 10, size=(50, 1))              # feature: hours studied
y = 3 * X[:, 0] + 2 + rng.normal(0, 1, size=50)   # target: score, with noise

model = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)              # training = fitting parameters
print(round(model.coef_[0], 2), round(model.intercept_, 2))  # 3.05 1.76 ← close to the true 3 and 2
print(round(model.predict([[4.0]])[0], 1))                   # 14.0 ← inference on a new input (true value 14)
The supervised setting
y = f(x) + ε learn f̂ ≈ f from pairs (x₁, y₁), …, (xₙ, yₙ)
f is the unknown true relationship and ε is noise no model can predict (measurement error, missing information). Even a perfect f̂ keeps the error caused by ε — the irreducible error. This picture covers supervised learning; unsupervised learning has no y at all (see Types of Learning).
Vocabulary
Term	Meaning	House-price example
Example / sample / row	One observation	One house sale
Feature (input, predictor) x	A measurable input variable	Area, bedrooms, distance to metro
Label / target y	The value to predict (supervised learning only)	Sale price
Model	A function f̂ from features to predictions	price ≈ w₁·area + w₂·bedrooms + b
Parameters	Learned from data during training	The weights w₁, w₂, b
Hyperparameters	Chosen before training, tuned on validation data	Regularisation strength, tree depth
Training / fitting	Adjusting parameters to reduce a loss on training data	model.fit(X, y)
Inference / prediction	Applying the trained model to new inputs	model.predict(X_new)
Generalisation	Performing well on unseen data from the same distribution	Accurate prices for next month''s listings
AI, ML and deep learning
Artificial Intelligence → Machine Learning → Deep Learning
Field	What it is	Example
Artificial intelligence	Any technique that makes machines act intelligently — including search, logic and planning that learn nothing from data	A route planner using A* search
Machine learning	The part of AI that learns behaviour from data	Gradient-boosted trees predicting customer churn
Deep learning	ML with many-layered neural networks that learn their own features from raw data	A transformer transcribing speech
Key point
These notes cover classic ML on mostly tabular data. Neural networks are covered in Deep Learning, LLMs and prompting in Generative AI, search/logic/planning in Artificial Intelligence, and NumPy/pandas/plotting in Data Science.
When ML is — and isn''t — the right tool
Do
Use ML when rules are too many or unknown (spam, fraud, images), the patterns change over time, there is enough representative data, and occasional errors are acceptable.
Avoid
Using ML when a few clear rules work, when there is no data (or no labels) to learn from, when every decision must be exactly explainable and guaranteed, or when a wrong answer is catastrophic and cannot be caught.
The workflow at a glance
Frame the problem → Collect & explore data → Split train / validation / test → Prepare features → Train a baseline, then better models → Tune & validate → Test once → Deploy & monitor
Each box has its own topic in these notes. The split comes before any preprocessing is fitted, and the test set is used once at the end — breaking either rule causes data leakage.
What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?
Parameters are learned from the training data (regression weights, tree split thresholds). Hyperparameters are set before training and control how learning happens (learning rate, max_depth, regularisation strength C); they are chosen by comparing models on validation data or with cross-validation.
What does it mean for a model to generalise?
It performs about as well on new data from the same distribution as on the data it was trained on. We estimate this with held-out data the model never saw during training or tuning. A model that is excellent on training data but poor on held-out data has overfit.
How is ML different from traditional programming?
Traditional programs apply rules a developer wrote. ML programs apply rules (parameters) that an algorithm fitted from examples, so behaviour comes from the data — which is also why data quality, representativeness and leakage matter so much.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'What Is Machine Learning?'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'What Is Machine Learning?'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Machine learning builds programs that improve at a task from data instead of from hand-written rules. You supply examples; a learning algorithm finds the parameters of a model that generalises to examples it has never seen.
