INSERT INTO "notes"."NoteSearchDocument" ("noteId", "versionId", "title", "topics", "body", "document", "updatedAt")
SELECT 'b32fe18f-eb78-7b5b-9a01-4d8b177f58a3', '044b1000-d8d8-7c9c-b912-ff12e1bc8515', 'Error Analysis', 'Error Analysis', 'Aggregate metrics say how much a model is wrong; error analysis finds where and why, so the next hour of work goes to the change that helps most.
First: which kind of error?
Compare	Gap means	Typical fixes
Human-level (≈ Bayes) error → training error	Avoidable bias	Bigger/more flexible model, better features, train longer, less regularisation
Training → validation error	Variance (overfitting)	More data, regularisation, simpler model, augmentation
Validation on training-like data → validation on target data	Data mismatch	Collect data like production, re-weight, domain features
Validation → test error	Overfitting the validation set (too many experiments)	Bigger/fresh validation set, fewer comparisons
Example
Human error 1%, training 8%, validation 10% → avoidable bias 7 points dominates variance 2 points: work on the model''s capacity or features, not on regularisation.
The procedure
Collect the errors
Take ~100 misclassified validation examples (or the worst regression residuals). Also look at the most confidently wrong ones.
Tag them
Read each one and tag the apparent cause: blurry image, sarcasm, missing feature, rare category, label error…
Count
A spreadsheet of tag frequencies shows where the errors concentrate.
Prioritise
Expected gain ≈ share of errors in the category × how fixable it is. Fixing 5% of errors can at most gain 5% of the error rate — the ceiling.
Act and repeat
Targeted data collection, features, relabelling or a separate model for a segment; then re-measure.
Slice the metrics
An overall score can hide a segment where the model fails. Compute metrics per slice — country, device, new vs returning users, time period:
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
n = 3000
segment = rng.choice(["returning", "new"], size=n, p=[0.85, 0.15])
x = rng.normal(size=(n, 3))
logit = 2 * x[:, 0] - x[:, 1]
logit[segment == "new"] = 2 * x[segment == "new", 2]     # new users behave differently
y = (rng.random(n) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-logit))).astype(int)

model = LogisticRegression().fit(x[:2000], y[:2000])
test = pd.DataFrame({"segment": segment[2000:], "correct": model.predict(x[2000:]) == y[2000:]})
print(round(test["correct"].mean(), 3))
print(test.groupby("segment")["correct"].agg(["mean", "size"]).round(3))
0.778
            mean  size
segment
new        0.541   146
returning  0.819   854
The overall accuracy looks acceptable, yet for new users the model is barely better than a coin flip — invisible without slicing. Slicing is also the basis of fairness audits (see Fairness, Privacy & Responsible AI).
Which classes get confused?
import numpy as np
from sklearn.datasets import load_digits
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict
from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

X, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)
pred = cross_val_predict(make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000)), X, y, cv=5)
cm = confusion_matrix(y, pred)
np.fill_diagonal(cm, 0)                                   # keep only the mistakes
top = np.dstack(np.unravel_index(np.argsort(cm.ravel())[::-1][:3], cm.shape))[0]
print([(int(a), int(b), int(cm[a, b])) for a, b in top])  # [(8, 1, 13), (3, 8, 12), (7, 9, 12)] ← (true, predicted, count): most frequent confusions
cross_val_predict gives every row an out-of-fold prediction — ideal for error analysis without touching the test set.
Checklist
Are some “errors” actually label mistakes? Confidently wrong predictions often are — fix labels in the evaluation set first.
Do errors cluster in a slice, a time period or a data source (a new app version, one sensor)?
Is there a feature the model would need but doesn''t have?
Are errors near the decision threshold (threshold/calibration issue) or far from it (model/data issue)?
Your model''s accuracy is 85%. How do you decide what to improve?
Compare training, validation and (if available) human-level error to see whether bias or variance dominates. Then do error analysis: sample misclassified validation examples, tag causes, count them, and estimate the ceiling gain per category; slice metrics by key segments. Work on the largest, most fixable source first.
What is slice-based evaluation?
Computing metrics separately for meaningful subgroups (region, device, demographic, data source). It reveals failures hidden by the average and is required for fairness and reliability checks before deployment.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Error Analysis'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Error Analysis'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Aggregate metrics say how much a model is wrong; error analysis finds where and why, so the next hour of work goes to the change that helps most.
First: which kind of error?
Compare	Gap means	Typical fixes
Human-level (≈ Bayes) error → training error	Avoidable bias	Bigger/more flexible model, better features, train longer, less regularisation
Training → validation error	Variance (overfitting)	More data, regularisation, simpler model, augmentation
Validation on training-like data → validation on target data	Data mismatch	Collect data like production, re-weight, domain features
Validation → test error	Overfitting the validation set (too many experiments)	Bigger/fresh validation set, fewer comparisons
Example
Human error 1%, training 8%, validation 10% → avoidable bias 7 points dominates variance 2 points: work on the model''s capacity or features, not on regularisation.
The procedure
Collect the errors
Take ~100 misclassified validation examples (or the worst regression residuals). Also look at the most confidently wrong ones.
Tag them
Read each one and tag the apparent cause: blurry image, sarcasm, missing feature, rare category, label error…
Count
A spreadsheet of tag frequencies shows where the errors concentrate.
Prioritise
Expected gain ≈ share of errors in the category × how fixable it is. Fixing 5% of errors can at most gain 5% of the error rate — the ceiling.
Act and repeat
Targeted data collection, features, relabelling or a separate model for a segment; then re-measure.
Slice the metrics
An overall score can hide a segment where the model fails. Compute metrics per slice — country, device, new vs returning users, time period:
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
n = 3000
segment = rng.choice(["returning", "new"], size=n, p=[0.85, 0.15])
x = rng.normal(size=(n, 3))
logit = 2 * x[:, 0] - x[:, 1]
logit[segment == "new"] = 2 * x[segment == "new", 2]     # new users behave differently
y = (rng.random(n) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-logit))).astype(int)

model = LogisticRegression().fit(x[:2000], y[:2000])
test = pd.DataFrame({"segment": segment[2000:], "correct": model.predict(x[2000:]) == y[2000:]})
print(round(test["correct"].mean(), 3))
print(test.groupby("segment")["correct"].agg(["mean", "size"]).round(3))
0.778
            mean  size
segment
new        0.541   146
returning  0.819   854
The overall accuracy looks acceptable, yet for new users the model is barely better than a coin flip — invisible without slicing. Slicing is also the basis of fairness audits (see Fairness, Privacy & Responsible AI).
Which classes get confused?
import numpy as np
from sklearn.datasets import load_digits
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_predict
from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

X, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)
pred = cross_val_predict(make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000)), X, y, cv=5)
cm = confusion_matrix(y, pred)
np.fill_diagonal(cm, 0)                                   # keep only the mistakes
top = np.dstack(np.unravel_index(np.argsort(cm.ravel())[::-1][:3], cm.shape))[0]
print([(int(a), int(b), int(cm[a, b])) for a, b in top])  # [(8, 1, 13), (3, 8, 12), (7, 9, 12)] ← (true, predicted, count): most frequent confusions
cross_val_predict gives every row an out-of-fold prediction — ideal for error analysis without touching the test set.
Checklist
Are some “errors” actually label mistakes? Confidently wrong predictions often are — fix labels in the evaluation set first.
Do errors cluster in a slice, a time period or a data source (a new app version, one sensor)?
Is there a feature the model would need but doesn''t have?
Are errors near the decision threshold (threshold/calibration issue) or far from it (model/data issue)?
Your model''s accuracy is 85%. How do you decide what to improve?
Compare training, validation and (if available) human-level error to see whether bias or variance dominates. Then do error analysis: sample misclassified validation examples, tag causes, count them, and estimate the ceiling gain per category; slice metrics by key segments. Work on the largest, most fixable source first.
What is slice-based evaluation?
Computing metrics separately for meaningful subgroups (region, device, demographic, data source). It reveals failures hidden by the average and is required for fairness and reliability checks before deployment.'), 'C'),
