  ('95a64c45-20f8-74d6-be01-4666bc5fc8cf', 'a0', 'text', '{"doc":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Short, precise answers to the machine-learning questions asked most often — each points back to the topic that explains it in depth."}]}]}}'),
  ('871cab0d-bdfc-7459-928c-f9a917666047', 'a1', 'heading', '{"level":1,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fundamentals"}]}'),
  ('51082443-79c7-77fd-b119-5c715cb3f7d8', 'a2', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the difference between supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Supervised learns a mapping from inputs to known labels (classification, regression). Unsupervised finds structure without labels (clustering, dimensionality reduction). Reinforcement learning learns a policy from rewards received by acting in an environment. (See "},{"type":"text","text":"Types of Learning","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":".)"}]}]}}'),
  ('84fc216c-b28f-7d48-b024-36e7c3018e3a', 'a3', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is overfitting and how do you prevent it?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The model learns noise in the training data, so training error is low but validation error is high. Prevent it with more data, simpler models, regularisation (L1/L2, pruning, early stopping), feature selection, bagging, and by validating properly with held-out data or cross-validation."}]}]}}'),
  ('28492641-79e9-7242-abd4-0745edd6429a', 'a4', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explain the bias–variance trade-off."}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expected error = bias² + variance + irreducible noise. Simple models have high bias and low variance (underfit); flexible models the reverse (overfit). Model complexity is tuned to minimise the sum, using validation data. (See "},{"type":"text","text":"Bias–Variance & Overfitting","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":".)"}]}]}}'),
  ('5b47c4a9-69dd-74fd-8caa-de9125a2f321', 'a5', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why do we split data into train, validation and test sets?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Train fits parameters, validation guides choices (hyperparameters, features, threshold), and the untouched test set gives an unbiased final estimate. Using the test set for choices makes its score optimistic."}]}]}}'),
  ('ee34a35e-d74b-713b-afbe-82892c04e246', 'a6', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is cross-validation and when would you use GroupKFold or TimeSeriesSplit?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rotating held-out folds to estimate performance more reliably. GroupKFold when several rows belong to the same entity (patient, user) so an entity never appears on both sides; TimeSeriesSplit when data is time-ordered so models are always validated on the future."}]}]}}'),
  ('82384a66-7d29-731f-941b-96f47ef1dbbe', 'a7', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is data leakage? Give two examples."}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Information unavailable at prediction time influencing training or evaluation. Examples: scaling or selecting features on the full dataset before splitting; a feature derived from the target (e.g. "},{"type":"text","text":"refund_issued","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" for fraud); the same customer in train and test. (See "},{"type":"text","text":"Data Leakage","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":".)"}]}]}}'),
  ('3d5c9228-fcbf-7138-9f43-732ba56dc8cc', 'a8', 'heading', '{"level":1,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Algorithms"}]}'),
  ('0e9caf29-009b-7dfa-b6df-43baa1a200da', 'a9', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How does logistic regression work?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It computes a linear score "},{"type":"text","text":"w·x + b","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and passes it through the sigmoid to get P(y = 1). It''s trained by minimising log-loss (maximum likelihood), a convex problem. The decision boundary is linear; coefficients are changes in log-odds."}]}]}}'),
  ('5f7db4aa-779a-7de8-89d8-2a4559d1a695', 'aA', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"L1 vs L2 regularisation?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"L1 (Lasso) adds "},{"type":"text","text":"α·Σ|w|","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and drives many weights exactly to zero — built-in feature selection. L2 (Ridge) adds "},{"type":"text","text":"α·Σw²","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and shrinks weights smoothly, handling correlated features well. Elastic Net combines them. Scale features first."}]}]}}'),
  ('6d6611ad-ac2c-7809-adb1-b960f1eb2bc4', 'aB', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How does a decision tree choose splits, and why do trees overfit?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Greedily, by the largest impurity decrease (Gini/entropy for classification, squared error for regression). Grown without limits a tree isolates individual points; limit depth or leaf size, prune, or use ensembles."}]}]}}'),
  ('ba7c3a21-9e2b-734e-a779-8429160fc36b', 'aC', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Random forest vs gradient boosting?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A random forest averages many deep trees trained independently on bootstrap samples with random feature subsets — it reduces variance, is robust and needs little tuning. Gradient boosting adds shallow trees sequentially, each fitting the gradient of the loss of the current ensemble — it reduces bias and is usually more accurate but needs a learning rate and early stopping to avoid overfitting."}]}]}}'),
  ('7c01a9b3-9e58-7588-9c60-4776689f7532', 'aD', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do SVMs work? What is the kernel trick?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An SVM finds the hyperplane with the maximum margin; soft margins trade margin width against violations via "},{"type":"text","text":"C","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". The kernel trick replaces dot products with a kernel function, giving non-linear boundaries as if the data were mapped to a higher-dimensional space."}]}]}}'),
  ('fdda5a7d-2ac4-7f6c-983f-4c09bb868a4d', 'aE', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How does k-NN work and what are its weaknesses?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It predicts from the k nearest training points (vote or average). No training, but slow predictions, needs feature scaling, and suffers from irrelevant features and the curse of dimensionality."}]}]}}'),
  ('ced3c298-4923-737e-a3a8-0a630065a471', 'aF', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is Naive Bayes naive, and why does it still work?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It assumes features are conditionally independent given the class. The probabilities it outputs are poorly calibrated, but the ranking of classes is often right, so classification works well — especially for text."}]}]}}'),
  ('73f2dd50-b52c-7379-8181-05983cbd769f', 'aG', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How does k-means work and what are its assumptions?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alternately assign points to the nearest centroid and recompute centroids, minimising within-cluster squared distances. Assumes roughly spherical, similarly sized clusters and meaningful Euclidean distances; needs k, scaling and several initialisations."}]}]}}'),
  ('9edc2ea7-1443-726e-a34d-122a484ae1e8', 'aH', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is PCA and why scale before it?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PCA projects data onto orthogonal directions of maximum variance (eigenvectors of the covariance matrix). Without scaling, features with large units dominate the variance and therefore the components."}]}]}}'),
